k of knowledge that enhances the understanding of nutrition's role in health. This interconnectedness facilitates the dissemination of research findings and promotes collaboration among researchers. [Data: Relationships (16571, 16570, 16572, +more)]\"\r\n446,\"# Cancer Nutrition Research Community\n\nThe Cancer Nutrition Research Community is centered around key entities involved in the study of nutritional support for cancer patients, including researchers and journals that publish significant findings in this field. The community is interconnected through various research collaborations and publications, highlighting the importance of nutrition in cancer treatment and outcomes.\n\n## Ireton-Jones' contributions to cancer nutrition\n\nIreton-Jones is a prominent researcher known for developing the Ireton-Jones equation, which estimates energy expenditure in patients, particularly those with specific medical conditions like cancer. This equation is crucial for determining the nutritional needs of critically ill patients, emphasizing the importance of tailored nutritional support in oncology. Ireton-Jones' work is widely recognized and has been published in various journals, contributing significantly to the understanding of nutritional requirements in cancer care [Data: Entities (4817); Relationships (5861)].\n\n## PLOS ONE's role in disseminating cancer research\n\nPLOS ONE is a multidisciplinary journal that publishes a wide range of research, including studies on nutrition and cancer. Its commitment to open access allows for broad dissemination of important findings, making it a vital platform for researchers in the field. Notably, PLOS ONE has published significant studies related to swallowing disorders in cancer patients, highlighting its relevance to the community's focus on nutritional outcomes [Data: Entities (223); Relationships (2126, 2193)].\n\n## BMC Cancer's focus on nutritional outcomes\n\nBMC Cancer is dedicated to publishing research related to cancer, including studies on nutritional outcomes for patients. The journal has featured the PROPILS trial, which investigates preoperative immunonutrition in liver resection for cancer, showcasing its commitment to advancing knowledge in this critical area. BMC Cancer serves as a key resource for researchers and clinicians looking to understand the impact of nutrition on cancer treatment and patient quality of life [Data: Entities (7687); Relationships (16547, 14096)].\n\n## The significance of the PROPILS trial\n\nThe PROPILS trial is a multicenter randomized controlled trial studying the effects of preoperative immunonutrition in patients undergoing liver resection for cancer. This trial is significant as it aims to provide evidence on how nutritional interventions can improve surgical outcomes and overall patient care. The trial's findings are expected to influence clinical practices and guidelines regarding nutritional support in oncology [Data: Entities (14999); Relationships (16547)].\n\n## Interconnectedness of research entities\n\nThe community is characterized by a high degree of interconnectedness among researchers and journals. For instance, multiple researchers involved in the PROPILS trial have their work published in BMC Cancer, indicating collaborative efforts to advance the field of cancer nutrition. This interconnectedness enhances the sharing of knowledge and resources, ultimately benefiting patient care and research outcomes [Data: Relationships (16553, 16560, 16561)].\n\n## Diverse research topics in cancer nutrition\n\nThe community encompasses a variety of research topics related to cancer nutrition, including dietary factors affecting cancer risk and the role of nutrition in treatment outcomes. Journals like Nutrition Cancer and Cancer Causes & Control publish studies that explore these relationships, contributing to a comprehensive understanding of how nutrition impacts cancer prevention and management. This diversity of research is crucial for 