y and quality across its member countries. This regulatory framework is essential for maintaining consumer trust and ensuring that products meet specific health and safety standards. [Data: Entities (7881); Relationships (4117, 16449)]\n\n## Certification of organic eggs\n\nEggs must be certified by the European Organic Certification Entity to be labeled as organic according to EU standards. This certification process ensures that eggs meet stringent organic farming practices, which are crucial for consumer health and environmental sustainability. The relationship between eggs and the certification entity highlights the importance of compliance with organic standards in the food industry. [Data: Entities (7974, 7975); Relationships (16919)]\n\n## Quality labels and their significance\n\nQuality labels such as Denomination of Origin Protected, Indication of Geographical Protected, and Traditional Specialty Guaranteed are regulated by the European Union to ensure that products meet specific quality standards. These labels not only enhance consumer awareness but also protect the reputation of products associated with particular geographical areas or traditional production methods. The presence of these labels indicates a commitment to quality and authenticity in food production. [Data: Entities (7976, 7977, 7978); Relationships (16811, 16812, 16813)]\n\n## The role of control bodies in certification\n\nControl bodies are integral to the certification process, ensuring that products meet organic standards and regulations set by the European Union. These organizations play a critical role in maintaining the integrity of organic labeling, which is essential for consumer confidence and market access for organic products. The relationship between the control body and the European Organic Certification Entity emphasizes the collaborative effort required to uphold food safety standards. [Data: Entities (7980); Relationships (16920)]\"\r\n\n\nThe report should include the foll